<!--BlockML Documentation — a guide to nested Block definitions

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.SubBlocks-->
<docs:SubBlocks xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation"
  xmlns:def="org.blockml.bml.definition">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A guide to nested Block definitions
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: nested Block definitions, Sub-Block identity and scope, and nesting depth, grounded in
    the Sub-Block sections of org.blockml.bml.documentation.SubBlockModel and
    org.blockml.bml.definition.SubBlockDefinition. Body content is authored in a03 from those
    sources.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="doc:SubBlockModel" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — Sub-Block nesting, identity derivation, and generic scope.
    </see>
    <see type="def:SubBlockDefinition" relation="Requires">
      The nested Sub-Block definition shape this page introduces.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>Sub-Blocks</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Introductory</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Concept</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <!--1. Lead-->
        <dpage:Section heading="A Block definition nested inside another">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A Sub-Block is a Block definition nested inside a parent Block, comparable to a
static nested class: it shares the parent's identity prefix without opening a
document or library of its own. A Sub-Block is declared inside the parent
Block rather than as its own document.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--2. Identity-->
        <dpage:Section heading="How a Sub-Block gets its identity">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A Sub-Block's local name is required, while its full identity is normally
derived by extending the immediately enclosing Block's identity with that
local name, so nesting can continue to any depth. A parent Block may itself
be a Sub-Block, so nesting composes to any depth.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--3. Declaring one-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Declaring a Sub-Block">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A Sub-Block is declared inside its parent Block, and a Sub-Block must always
state its own baseType even though its other fields follow the same shape as
a root Block.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--4. Generic scope-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Sub-Blocks and generic type parameters">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A Sub-Block may declare its own generic type parameters, but it does not see
the generic type parameters of its enclosing Block — a parameter the parent
needs inside the Sub-Block must be forwarded explicitly.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--5. Recap-->
        <dpage:Section heading="What to remember about Sub-Blocks">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A Sub-Block lets a Block definition nest inside a parent while sharing its
identity prefix, with deeper nesting simply extending that prefix one more
step; a different kind of nested document, the embedded Block, follows next.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--6. Continuation-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with embedded Blocks">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="Embedded Blocks" summary="See how an embedded Block differs from a Sub-Block by keeping its own independent identity.">
                  <target>docs:EmbeddedBlocks</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:SubBlocks>
